+# Daily Grind rewards
+
+Node.js 24+ and pnpm are required. Install with `pnpm install`, check with `pnpm run build`, and run the automated suite with `pnpm test`. Tests call the request handler directly and use generated cryptographic fixtures; they do not open a listener or import `server.mjs`.
+
+The homepage now contains a native rewards registration form. `POST /api/verify-email` verifies browser-provided Email Verification Protocol (EVP) presentations and creates the account only after successful verification. Passwords are salted and hashed with scrypt. Accounts and single-use registration challenges are stored in SQLite at `data/rewards.sqlite`; persist this directory across deployments. Set `DATABASE_PATH` to use another persistent path (its parent directory must exist). The page and responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+
+## Deployment configuration
+
+- `PUBLIC_ORIGIN`: the exact public HTTPS origin, with no path or trailing slash; this is the expected token audience and POST origin. Defaults to `http://localhost:11222` for local setup, adjusted by `PORT`.
+- `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`: a valid relying-party origin-trial token for that public origin, supplied via the homepage's `Origin-Trial` response header. Obtain it through the [Chrome EVP origin trial](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/email-verification-protocol-origin-trial). There is no placeholder or fabricated trial credential in the source.
+- `PORT`: HTTP listener port, default `11222`. The deployment must terminate HTTPS in front of the Node process.
+- `DATABASE_PATH`: optional persistent SQLite filename.
+
+`pnpm start` is the production server command. A static file host alone cannot run registration. No server was launched during implementation.
+
+The browser reads the hidden `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` input in the same form as the email field. Its nonce is generated on the server and linked to an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax cookie. Submission atomically consumes that challenge, including on failed attempts. Verification uses `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose` for issuer and holder signatures, email ownership, freshness, origin, nonce, and SD hash binding. Email-domain DNS delegation must match the issuer before discovery. Discovery and JWKS retrieval allow only public HTTPS endpoints, pin DNS resolution, disallow redirects, and limit response time and size.
+
+EVP is experimental and depends on browser, provider, active email-provider session, HTTPS, and origin-trial support. Unsupported or empty-token submissions show a retry explanation and never create an unverified account. As requested, no OTP or magic-link fallback is sent. Successfully creating an account does not sign the customer in; existing account navigation remains outside this registration change.
+
+Rate limits use the direct socket IP and process-local counters; a production reverse proxy should apply distributed rate limits at its edge. SQLite challenges and uniqueness constraints work across processes sharing the same database on a single host. Configure durable shared storage before distributing the app across separate hosts.
